caveat about their reliability.","category":"income","matters":"Planning income is discretionary and sensitive to economic conditions; waste budgets face recycling market volatility; both are flagged as materially at risk despite current performance","figures":"Currently forecast to achieve targets but volatility acknowledged","quote":"whilst planning income and waste budgets are currently forecasted to achieve their targets, these areas remain subject to market and operational factors, and changes in demand","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://rds.eppingforestdc.gov.uk/documents/s129832/129832.pdf","headline":"Local government reorganisation costs flagged as financial risk with unknown quantum","detail":"Local government reorganisation costs are listed among financial risks but the report does not quantify them or provide timing. This represents an emerging cost pressure of uncertain scale.","category":"other","matters":"LG restructuring could be material cost to absorb; unknown impact complicates medium-term financial planning and adds to downside risk","figures":"Not quantified","quote":"financial risks remain, including homelessness demand, commercial property income, local government reorganisation costs, and wider economic uncertainties","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://rds.eppingforestdc.gov.uk/documents/s129832/129832.pdf","headline":"Medium-term financial plan identifies structural budget gaps rising from \u00a31.54m to \u00a32.69m","detail":"The MTFP identifies a budget gap of \u00a31.54m in 2027/28, rising to \u00a32.69m by 2028/29. The report notes that further savings, income generation or other measures will be required to achieve a balanced budget over the planning period.","category":"savings","matters":"Structural budget gaps of this scale mean the council needs to identify unbudgeted savings within two years; failure would require further reserve drawdowns or borrowing","figures":"\u00a31.54m in 2027/28, rising to \u00a32.69m in 2028/29","quote":"the current MTFP identifies a budget gap of \u00a31.54m in 2027/28, rising to \u00a32.69m by 2028/29","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://rds.eppingforestdc.gov.uk/documents/s129832/129832.pdf","headline":"General Fund reserves held at approved minimum level with no headroom","detail":"The report forecasts General Fund balance maintained at \u00a34.0m, which is described as the 'approved minimum level'. This leaves no buffer above the minimum to absorb adverse variances or unexpected costs.","category":"reserves","matters":"No headroom above minimum reserve means adverse variances would immediately require service cuts or policy revision; significantly limits financial flexibility and resilience","figures":"\u00a34.0m minimum level","quote":"General Fund balance forecast to be maintained at the approved minimum level of \u00a34.0m","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Budget Monitoring Report - 2026/27 Quarter 1","paper_url":"https://rds.eppingforestdc.gov.uk/documents/s129832/129832.pdf","headline":"Bad debt provisions are increasing, offsetting planned savings","detail":"Rising bad debt provisions are listed among the budget pressures in Q1 that offset strong planning income and capital financing savings. This suggests deteriorating creditworthiness of debtors.","category":"income","matters":"Rising provisions indicate collection risk and service user hardship; signals deteriorating ability or willingness to pay across services","figures":"Included in Q1 pressures but not separately quantified","quote":"pressures in commercial property income, homelessness, and bad debt provisions","material":true}],"editorial":null,